From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_pr_fixes_use <>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:59:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] os/mm: Say in Kconfig that a reallocated chunk is not covered

The option is called use-after-free detection, which sets an
expectation it does not meet in the case most engineers picture: one
thread frees a block, a second thread allocates the same block, and the
first thread then writes through its stale pointer.

The detector only watches a chunk while it is on the free list. Once
the chunk has been handed out again the address legitimately belongs to
the new owner, so no content check can tell the stale write apart from
a valid one. Catching that needs the reuse of the chunk to be delayed,
which this option does not do.

State the limit in the help text so that a reader knows what is and is
not covered before enabling it.
---
 os/Kconfig.debug | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/os/Kconfig.debug b/os/Kconfig.debug
index df49b6e8..defcc7b1 100644
--- a/os/Kconfig.debug
+++ b/os/Kconfig.debug
@@ -799,6 +799,14 @@ config DEBUG_MM_UAF
 		chunk is handed out by malloc. A mismatch means somebody wrote into
 		the chunk after it was freed (use-after-free).
 
+		Not covered: a chunk which has already been handed out again. The
+		detector only watches a chunk while it sits on the free list. If one
+		thread frees a block, a second thread allocates the same block, and
+		the first thread then writes through its stale pointer, the write
+		lands in memory which legitimately belongs to the second thread and
+		nothing reports it. That case needs the reuse of the chunk to be
+		delayed, which this option does not do.
+
 		Not covered: the free node bookkeeping at the start of the user data,
 		from offset 0 up to SIZEOF_MM_FREENODE. The allocator reuses that
 		region for flink, blink and the free debug info while the chunk is on
